{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KctRz3mQzDGsXC1xAGQqkT","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Under AdamW with a fixed learning rate, a standard-parametrized transformer's per-step function-space change grows with width (coherent sum over d inputs), so wider models memorize faster (smaller t_mem) and overshoot into the lazy memorizing solution; apparent width effects on grokking delay (t_gen - t_mem) are therefore partly an effective-learning-rate confound rather than a capacity effect. Falsifiable predictions: (P1) Re-expressing each hidden matrix as W = m * V with m = (REF_WIDTH/d)**HIDDEN_EXP (V initialised at std/m, so Adam moves W at rate lr*m while decoupled weight decay on V keeps the same relative decay rate) will flatten the slope of log t_mem vs log width compared with HIDDEN_EXP=0 (|slope| shrinks by a clearly non-zero margin, bootstrap CI over seeds excluding equality). (P2) If the confound explains the width dependence of delay, the slope of log(delay) vs log(width) also moves toward 0 under HIDDEN_EXP=1; if t_mem flattens but delay slope does not change, the effective-LR explanation is refuted and the width dependence is carried by t_gen (circuit-efficiency/capacity). (P3) Censoring (no generalization within the step budget) should drop for the widest and narrowest models under the compensated parametrization at fixed weight decay. The conclusion is conditional on single-layer transformer, modular addition, AdamW, full batch; failure cases (no grokking at any width, all runs censored, or no change in slope) are reported as-is.\",\n  \"idea_id\": \"c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound\",\n  \"implementation_plan\": [\n    \"Start from the best program (program_1 of cycle 2: last-position-only forward, INIT_SCALE, REF_WIDTH/OUT_EXP output scaling); keep the python_rpc_v1 ops, response keys and honoring of evaluator-owned hyperparameters (AdamW lr, betas, weight_decay, full batch, max_steps) unchanged.\",\n    \"Add module constants HIDDEN_EXP (default moderate, e.g. 0.5; 0 reproduces cycle-2 behaviour) and a per-matrix multiplier self.hid_mult = (REF_WIDTH / d) ** HIDDEN_EXP applied to qkv, proj, fc1, fc2 in forward via reparametrization W = hid_mult * V, with V initialised divided by hid_mult so the function at init is unchanged.\",\n    \"Implement the multiplier inside forward (e.g. w = self.qkv.weight * self.hid_mult; F.linear(h, self.fc1.weight * self.hid_mult, self.fc1.bias)) so a single AdamW with the evaluator's hyperparameters is used; no parameter groups with custom lr, no label use beyond cross-entropy on loaded rows, no hard-coded Fourier/cyclic features.\",\n    \"Extend src/grok_sweep.py (offline, not called by evaluator) with a hidden_exp switch: run widths >=4 values x >=3 seeds x hidden_exp in {0, 0.5, 1} at a fixed train fraction under AdamW, recording t_mem (first step train_acc>=0.99), t_gen (first step val_acc>=0.99), delay, censored flag.\",\n    \"In the sweep report bootstrap 95% CIs per width per hidden_exp, and fit slopes with np.polyfit(np.log(width), np.log(t), 1) for t_mem, t_gen, delay (uncensored runs only, with censored counts shown), and a bootstrap CI of slope difference between hidden_exp=0 and 1.\",\n    \"If execution is available, use the sweep to choose HIDDEN_EXP default; fall back to HIDDEN_EXP=0 if the compensated parametrization lowers final validation accuracy or increases censoring at the evaluator's widths.\",\n    \"Document in a README note the confound logic, the limitations (single seed-count budget, wd interplay, V-space decay differs from W-space decay by m**2 in decay-gradient terms), and which script produces each paper figure.\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_agent\": \"Cycle 1 (capacity sweep, init scale 0.5) scored 0.798 with censored_fraction 0.67; cycle 2 (output scaling OUT_EXP=0.5, UNEMBED_INIT=0.5) scored 0.828 with censored_fraction 1.0 and lower final_val_acc_mean (0.944 vs 0.981) -- the score formula is not visible, so do not assume lower censoring means higher score; the cause of the score change is unknown. Keep the cycle-2 program as the base and make HIDDEN_EXP an isolated change; if offline execution is possible, compare HIDDEN_EXP in {0,0.5,1} and keep the best by validation accuracy at evaluator widths, otherwise default to a mild 0.5. Candidate sees only train rows and the workload fields in reset; do not rely on losses/val labels. Note existing code defines _INIT_SCALE but the reset-time constants must remain consistent. Paper framing: conditional/negative results are fine; report censored runs honestly and never count them as successes.\",\n  \"target_rubrics\": [\n    {\n      \"description\": \"combined_score > 0.828466629206468\",\n      \"id\": \"incumbent_score_improvement\",\n      \"metric\": \"combined_score\",\n      \"operator\": \">\",\n      \"threshold\": 0.828466629206468,\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Candidate net rescales the hidden matrices (qkv, proj, fc1, fc2) via a width-dependent multiplier (REF_WIDTH/d_model)**HIDDEN_EXP applied in forward to the raw weights, with V initialised divided by the multiplier so initial function is unchanged; HIDDEN_EXP is a module-level constant, the evaluator's AdamW hyperparameters are used untouched (no custom parameter-group lr), and no modular-arithmetic structure is hard-coded.\",\n      \"id\": \"hidden_layer_reparametrization\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"HIDDEN_EXP\",\n          \"self.hid_mult =\",\n          \"* self.hid_mult\",\n          \"(REF_WIDTH / d) ** HIDDEN_EXP\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Offline script src/grok_sweep.py sweeps >=4 widths x >=3 seeds x hidden_exp values {0, 0.5, 1} under AdamW, reports t_mem, t_gen, delay and a censored flag with bootstrap 95% CIs, and fits log-log slopes of t_mem, t_gen and delay vs width per hidden_exp including a bootstrap CI of the slope difference.\",\n      \"id\": \"effective_lr_ablation_sweep\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"hidden_exp\",\n          \"censored\",\n          \"bootstrap\",\n          \"np.polyfit(np.log\",\n          \"t_mem\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"title\": \"Effective-Learning-Rate Confound: Width-Scaled Hidden-Layer Reparametrization and Grokking Delay\"\n}{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Grokking delay (steps from train-acc>=99% to val-acc>=99%) in modular arithmetic is set by the competition between two time scales: t_mem (time to memorize the n_train = f*p^2 training pairs), which shrinks quickly with width because wide nets have spare memorization capacity, and t_gen (time for the weight-decay-driven transition to the generalizing Fourier circuit), which depends only weakly on width. Falsifiable predictions (AdamW, fixed lr/wd, 1-layer transformer or embed+MLP, p=97, ops: add and a harder op such as x^2+xy+y^2 mod p): (P1) t_mem decreases monotonically with width d, with log-log slope clearly < 0, while t_gen has |slope| at least 2x smaller, so the delay (t_gen - t_mem) INCREASES with width over the range where memorization is possible. (P2) There is a critical width d*(f) below which the net cannot memorize (parameter count ~ n_train*log2 p bits): train acc never reaches 99%; d* grows approximately linearly in training fraction f (slope of log d* vs log f within [0.5, 1.5] of 1) -- so 'no grokking in small models' is a capacity failure, not an absence of the generalizing circuit. (P3) Just above d*, models fit train and generalize near-simultaneously (delay ~ 0), giving a hump-shaped delay vs. width curve (zero below d*, max just above, then plateau/decline) whose peak position shifts with f as in P2. (P4) Control: if weight decay is scaled so that t_gen is equalized across widths, the width trend in delay is explained by t_mem alone; if instead the delay is not increasing with width at fixed wd, P1 is refuted. Conclusions are reported with 5+ seeds per cell, bootstrap 95% CIs and rank-correlation tests; refutation outcomes (e.g. delay monotonically decreasing with width, as in a lazy-to-rich account where wide nets enrich sooner) are reported as-is.\",\n  \"idea_id\": \"c01_capacity_crossover_grok_width\",\n  \"implementation_plan\": [\n    \"Implement synthetic modular-arithmetic data in src/program.py: all p^2 pairs (a,b), op in {add, quad}, train fraction f split with fixed seeds; full-batch training on CPU.\",\n    \"Model: small transformer (1 layer, d_model in {16,32,64,128,256}, heads=4, MLP 4d) plus an embed+MLP variant; AdamW with lr=1e-3, wd=1.0, betas (0.9,0.98); log train/val acc and loss every K steps (K=50) for up to ~20k-50k steps with early stop 500 steps after val>=99%.\",\n    \"Define metrics: t_mem = first step train_acc>=0.99, t_gen = first step val_acc>=0.99 (censored at max steps, tracked with censor flag), delay = t_gen - t_mem; also record final-weight L2 norm and parameter count.\",\n    \"Experiment A (width sweep): widths x 5 seeds at f=0.4, p=97; report t_mem, t_gen, delay vs width with bootstrap 95% CI, log-log slope fits (P1) and hump-shape check (P3).\",\n    \"Experiment B (capacity boundary): grid width x f in {0.2,0.3,0.4,0.5,0.7} x 3-5 seeds; estimate d*(f) as the smallest width reaching train>=99% in majority of seeds; fit log d* vs log f slope with bootstrap CI (P2).\",\n    \"Experiment C (control): re-run width sweep with wd tuned per width to equalize t_gen (or sweep wd in {0.3,1,3}); test whether delay trend is explained by t_mem (P4). Optional second op to test scope.\",\n    \"Analysis script: aggregate JSON results to tables/figures (delay vs width, d* vs f, training curves), with censoring handled explicitly (report fraction of censored runs, no imputation as success).\",\n    \"Make program entrypoint emit a deterministic compact summary (per-cell medians, CIs, slopes) and save raw per-run logs; fix seeds and keep total budget within tens of CPU-hours (use a reduced-grid smoke mode).\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_agent\": \"Interface of program.py/evaluator is unknown to the idea stage: follow whatever entrypoint contract exists in the baseline program (src/program.py) and keep correctness first; the harness injects the combined_score > incumbent gate itself. Keep CPU-only and cheap: p=97 with full-batch training, d_model up to 256, 1 layer; use torch.set_num_threads and run seeds in parallel processes. Start with a small smoke grid to verify grokking appears (wd~1, lr~1e-3, f~0.4-0.5, 20k+ steps) before the full sweep; if grokking does not appear at all, tune wd/lr rather than fabricate. Report negative results honestly (e.g. monotone-decreasing delay with width refutes P1). Never fabricate numbers; all reported values must come from the runs. Log censored runs explicitly. Do not search for or read any paper specifically about model size and grokking beyond the starter literature.\",\n  \"target_rubrics\": [\n    {\n      \"description\": \"combined_score > 0.7633826559280763\",\n      \"id\": \"incumbent_score_improvement\",\n      \"metric\": \"combined_score\",\n      \"operator\": \">\",\n      \"threshold\": 0.7633826559280763,\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Runs compute t_mem (first step train_acc>=0.99), t_gen (first step val_acc>=0.99) and delay=t_gen-t_mem for every run, with an explicit censored flag when val_acc never reaches 0.99 within the step budget; censored runs are never counted as successes.\",\n      \"id\": \"delay_metric_with_censoring\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"t_mem\",\n          \"t_gen\",\n          \"censored\",\n          \"delay = t_gen - t_mem\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Code sweeps model width (at least 4 values) and training fraction (at least 4 values) with at least 3 seeds per cell using AdamW weight decay, and reports bootstrap 95% confidence intervals of delay, t_mem and t_gen per cell.\",\n      \"id\": \"width_by_fraction_sweep\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"torch.optim.AdamW\",\n          \"train_fraction\",\n          \"bootstrap\",\n          \"for seed in\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Code estimates the critical width d*(f) (smallest width reaching train acc>=0.99 in a majority of seeds) and fits log d* vs log f slope with a bootstrap CI, testing the capacity-crossover prediction P2.\",\n      \"id\": \"capacity_boundary_fit\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"critical_width\",\n          \"np.polyfit(np.log\"\n        ],","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1b3d8743-eb54-4312-8de4-ec74f69e2571","uuid":"adee1295-16db-46bd-aa22-9e88f2b3afa9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:10:32.865Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Under AdamW with a fixed learning rate, a standard-parametrized transformer's per-step function-space change grows with width (coherent sum over d inputs), so wider models memorize faster (smaller t_mem) and overshoot into the lazy memorizing solution; apparent width effects on grokking delay (t_gen - t_mem) are therefore partly an effective-learning-rate confound rather than a capacity effect. Falsifiable predictions: (P1) Re-expressing each hidden matrix as W = m * V with m = (REF_WIDTH/d)**HIDDEN_EXP (V initialised at std/m, so Adam moves W at rate lr*m while decoupled weight decay on V keeps the same relative decay rate) will flatten the slope of log t_mem vs log width compared with HIDDEN_EXP=0 (|slope| shrinks by a clearly non-zero margin, bootstrap CI over seeds excluding equality). (P2) If the confound explains the width dependence of delay, the slope of log(delay) vs log(width) also moves toward 0 under HIDDEN_EXP=1; if t_mem flattens but delay slope does not change, the effective-LR explanation is refuted and the width dependence is carried by t_gen (circuit-efficiency/capacity). (P3) Censoring (no generalization within the step budget) should drop for the widest and narrowest models under the compensated parametrization at fixed weight decay. The conclusion is conditional on single-layer transformer, modular addition, AdamW, full batch; failure cases (no grokking at any width, all runs censored, or no change in slope) are reported as-is.\",\n  \"idea_id\": \"c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound\",\n  \"implementation_plan\": [\n    \"Start from the best program (program_1 of cycle 2: last-position-only forward, INIT_SCALE, REF_WIDTH/OUT_EXP output scaling); keep the python_rpc_v1 ops, response keys and honoring of evaluator-owned hyperparameters (AdamW lr, betas, weight_decay, full batch, max_steps) unchanged.\",\n    \"Add module constants HIDDEN_EXP (default moderate, e.g. 0.5; 0 reproduces cycle-2 behaviour) and a per-matrix multiplier self.hid_mult = (REF_WIDTH / d) ** HIDDEN_EXP applied to qkv, proj, fc1, fc2 in forward via reparametrization W = hid_mult * V, with V initialised divided by hid_mult so the function at init is unchanged.\",\n    \"Implement the multiplier inside forward (e.g. w = self.qkv.weight * self.hid_mult; F.linear(h, self.fc1.weight * self.hid_mult, self.fc1.bias)) so a single AdamW with the evaluator's hyperparameters is used; no parameter groups with custom lr, no label use beyond cross-entropy on loaded rows, no hard-coded Fourier/cyclic features.\",\n    \"Extend src/grok_sweep.py (offline, not called by evaluator) with a hidden_exp switch: run widths >=4 values x >=3 seeds x hidden_exp in {0, 0.5, 1} at a fixed train fraction under AdamW, recording t_mem (first step train_acc>=0.99), t_gen (first step val_acc>=0.99), delay, censored flag.\",\n    \"In the sweep report bootstrap 95% CIs per width per hidden_exp, and fit slopes with np.polyfit(np.log(width), np.log(t), 1) for t_mem, t_gen, delay (uncensored runs only, with censored counts shown), and a bootstrap CI of slope difference between hidden_exp=0 and 1.\",\n    \"If execution is available, use the sweep to choose HIDDEN_EXP default; fall back to HIDDEN_EXP=0 if the compensated parametrization lowers final validation accuracy or increases censoring at the evaluator's widths.\",\n    \"Document in a README note the confound logic, the limitations (single seed-count budget, wd interplay, V-space decay differs from W-space decay by m**2 in decay-gradient terms), and which script produces each paper figure.\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_agent\": \"Cycle 1 (capacity sweep, init scale 0.5) scored 0.798 with censored_fraction 0.67; cycle 2 (output scaling OUT_EXP=0.5, UNEMBED_INIT=0.5) scored 0.828 with censored_fraction 1.0 and lower final_val_acc_mean (0.944 vs 0.981) -- the score formula is not visible, so do not assume lower censoring means higher score; the cause of the score change is unknown. Keep the cycle-2 program as the base and make HIDDEN_EXP an isolated change; if offline execution is possible, compare HIDDEN_EXP in {0,0.5,1} and keep the best by validation accuracy at evaluator widths, otherwise default to a mild 0.5. Candidate sees only train rows and the workload fields in reset; do not rely on losses/val labels. Note existing code defines _INIT_SCALE but the reset-time constants must remain consistent. Paper framing: conditional/negative results are fine; report censored runs honestly and never count them as successes.\",\n  \"target_rubrics\": [\n    {\n      \"description\": \"combined_score > 0.828466629206468\",\n      \"id\": \"incumbent_score_improvement\",\n      \"metric\": \"combined_score\",\n      \"operator\": \">\",\n      \"threshold\": 0.828466629206468,\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Candidate net rescales the hidden matrices (qkv, proj, fc1, fc2) via a width-dependent multiplier (REF_WIDTH/d_model)**HIDDEN_EXP applied in forward to the raw weights, with V initialised divided by the multiplier so initial function is unchanged; HIDDEN_EXP is a module-level constant, the evaluator's AdamW hyperparameters are used untouched (no custom parameter-group lr), and no modular-arithmetic structure is hard-coded.\",\n      \"id\": \"hidden_layer_reparametrization\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"HIDDEN_EXP\",\n          \"self.hid_mult =\",\n          \"* self.hid_mult\",\n          \"(REF_WIDTH / d) ** HIDDEN_EXP\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Offline script src/grok_sweep.py sweeps >=4 widths x >=3 seeds x hidden_exp values {0, 0.5, 1} under AdamW, reports t_mem, t_gen, delay and a censored flag with bootstrap 95% CIs, and fits log-log slopes of t_mem, t_gen and delay vs width per hidden_exp including a bootstrap CI of the slope difference.\",\n      \"id\": \"effective_lr_ablation_sweep\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"hidden_exp\",\n          \"censored\",\n          \"bootstrap\",\n          \"np.polyfit(np.log\",\n          \"t_mem\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"title\": \"Effective-Learning-Rate Confound: Width-Scaled Hidden-Layer Reparametrization and Grokking Delay\"\n}{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Grokking delay (steps from train-acc>=99% to val-acc>=99%) in modular arithmetic is set by the competition between two time scales: t_mem (time to memorize the n_train = f*p^2 training pairs), which shrinks quickly with width because wide nets have spare memorization capacity, and t_gen (time for the weight-decay-driven transition to the generalizing Fourier circuit), which depends only weakly on width. Falsifiable predictions (AdamW, fixed lr/wd, 1-layer transformer or embed+MLP, p=97, ops: add and a harder op such as x^2+xy+y^2 mod p): (P1) t_mem decreases monotonically with width d, with log-log slope clearly < 0, while t_gen has |slope| at least 2x smaller, so the delay (t_gen - t_mem) INCREASES with width over the range where memorization is possible. (P2) There is a critical width d*(f) below which the net cannot memorize (parameter count ~ n_train*log2 p bits): train acc never reaches 99%; d* grows approximately linearly in training fraction f (slope of log d* vs log f within [0.5, 1.5] of 1) -- so 'no grokking in small models' is a capacity failure, not an absence of the generalizing circuit. (P3) Just above d*, models fit train and generalize near-simultaneously (delay ~ 0), giving a hump-shaped delay vs. width curve (zero below d*, max just above, then plateau/decline) whose peak position shifts with f as in P2. (P4) Control: if weight decay is scaled so that t_gen is equalized across widths, the width trend in delay is explained by t_mem alone; if instead the delay is not increasing with width at fixed wd, P1 is refuted. Conclusions are reported with 5+ seeds per cell, bootstrap 95% CIs and rank-correlation tests; refutation outcomes (e.g. delay monotonically decreasing with width, as in a lazy-to-rich account where wide nets enrich sooner) are reported as-is.\",\n  \"idea_id\": \"c01_capacity_crossover_grok_width\",\n  \"implementation_plan\": [\n    \"Implement synthetic modular-arithmetic data in src/program.py: all p^2 pairs (a,b), op in {add, quad}, train fraction f split with fixed seeds; full-batch training on CPU.\",\n    \"Model: small transformer (1 layer, d_model in {16,32,64,128,256}, heads=4, MLP 4d) plus an embed+MLP variant; AdamW with lr=1e-3, wd=1.0, betas (0.9,0.98); log train/val acc and loss every K steps (K=50) for up to ~20k-50k steps with early stop 500 steps after val>=99%.\",\n    \"Define metrics: t_mem = first step train_acc>=0.99, t_gen = first step val_acc>=0.99 (censored at max steps, tracked with censor flag), delay = t_gen - t_mem; also record final-weight L2 norm and parameter count.\",\n    \"Experiment A (width sweep): widths x 5 seeds at f=0.4, p=97; report t_mem, t_gen, delay vs width with bootstrap 95% CI, log-log slope fits (P1) and hump-shape check (P3).\",\n    \"Experiment B (capacity boundary): grid width x f in {0.2,0.3,0.4,0.5,0.7} x 3-5 seeds; estimate d*(f) as the smallest width reaching train>=99% in majority of seeds; fit log d* vs log f slope with bootstrap CI (P2).\",\n    \"Experiment C (control): re-run width sweep with wd tuned per width to equalize t_gen (or sweep wd in {0.3,1,3}); test whether delay trend is explained by t_mem (P4). Optional second op to test scope.\",\n    \"Analysis script: aggregate JSON results to tables/figures (delay vs width, d* vs f, training curves), with censoring handled explicitly (report fraction of censored runs, no imputation as success).\",\n    \"Make program entrypoint emit a deterministic compact summary (per-cell medians, CIs, slopes) and save raw per-run logs; fix seeds and keep total budget within tens of CPU-hours (use a reduced-grid smoke mode).\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_agent\": \"Interface of program.py/evaluator is unknown to the idea stage: follow whatever entrypoint contract exists in the baseline program (src/program.py) and keep correctness first; the harness injects the combined_score > incumbent gate itself. Keep CPU-only and cheap: p=97 with full-batch training, d_model up to 256, 1 layer; use torch.set_num_threads and run seeds in parallel processes. Start with a small smoke grid to verify grokking appears (wd~1, lr~1e-3, f~0.4-0.5, 20k+ steps) before the full sweep; if grokking does not appear at all, tune wd/lr rather than fabricate. Report negative results honestly (e.g. monotone-decreasing delay with width refutes P1). Never fabricate numbers; all reported values must come from the runs. Log censored runs explicitly. Do not search for or read any paper specifically about model size and grokking beyond the starter literature.\",\n  \"target_rubrics\": [\n    {\n      \"description\": \"combined_score > 0.7633826559280763\",\n      \"id\": \"incumbent_score_improvement\",\n      \"metric\": \"combined_score\",\n      \"operator\": \">\",\n      \"threshold\": 0.7633826559280763,\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Runs compute t_mem (first step train_acc>=0.99), t_gen (first step val_acc>=0.99) and delay=t_gen-t_mem for every run, with an explicit censored flag when val_acc never reaches 0.99 within the step budget; censored runs are never counted as successes.\",\n      \"id\": \"delay_metric_with_censoring\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"t_mem\",\n          \"t_gen\",\n          \"censored\",\n          \"delay = t_gen - t_mem\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Code sweeps model width (at least 4 values) and training fraction (at least 4 values) with at least 3 seeds per cell using AdamW weight decay, and reports bootstrap 95% confidence intervals of delay, t_mem and t_gen per cell.\",\n      \"id\": \"width_by_fraction_sweep\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"torch.optim.AdamW\",\n          \"train_fraction\",\n          \"bootstrap\",\n          \"for seed in\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Code estimates the critical width d*(f) (smallest width reaching train acc>=0.99 in a majority of seeds) and fits log d* vs log f slope with a bootstrap CI, testing the capacity-crossover prediction P2.\",\n      \"id\": \"capacity_boundary_fit\",\n      \"type\": \"implemen